Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Brass Tripods Shiver at Aphelion
- Story & Context: When nineteen-year-old rail mechanic Ren bolted an iron Ō-daiko to the corrugated roof of the Omiya Shunting Yard, the vibrations cracked the plastic eyepiece of an amateur refractor telescope aimed at Saturn. Below them, forty insomniacs leaned out of the rattling 03:14 express windows, daisy-chaining pocket synthesizers through headphone splitters until the locomotive's suspension groaned in F-sharp minor. The transit guards climbed the water tower with bolt cutters, but the sub-frequencies rattled their clipboards into the ballast gravel before a single wire was cut. Ren dropped his velvet mallets into the steam exhaust and sang down the skylight, "we traded our tickets for an armful of comets."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: 35mm grainy photograph of a brass amateur telescope tripod trembling on a cold corrugated tin roof, focused on an ink-blue sky where Saturn glows faintly, with massive velvet-draped ceremonial drums and scattered audio cables resting in damp morning mist.

Track 2: "Did You Focus on the Nebula or the High-Line?"
- Story & Context: In the drafty attic above Ueno Junction, chronic sleepwalker Mariko admitted she had stopped pointing her homemade cardboard Newtonian reflector at Cygnus weeks ago, aiming it instead at the flickering green LEDs of passing sleeper cars. She sampled the iron clatter using a battery-powered micro-synth, stretching each rail-joint thud into an eight-second cloud of white muslin that drowned out her brother’s accusations of madness. A sudden short-circuit in her power bank scorched the linoleum with the smell of burnt cloves just as the coastal coal train rumbled beneath the floorboards. She pulled her wool cardigan tight against the chimney draft and whispered into a contact mic, "my retina only burns when the signal switches green."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Poetic cinematic artwork of a cracked cardboard telescope balanced against a brick chimney at night, framed by the blurry emerald-green lights of an elevated railway far below, ethereal smoke and dust particles glowing softly in cold ambient starlight.

Track 3: Yakei-Bachi on Cold Zinc
- Story & Context: By mid-winter, legend held that the insomniac railway union had secretly re-engineered the suspension of the entire Joban Line into a giant acoustic resonator that only sang when struck from tenement roofs. Stationmaster Kudo claimed he witnessed twenty hooded rooftop observers strike zinc flashing with bachi sticks wrapped in raw silk, perfectly synchronizing with the sub-bass drone of the empty 04:00 mail train. When the dawn commuter shift arrived, they found no rogue musicians—only miles of frozen steel rails vibrating so intensely that spilled thermoses of sweet oolong tea danced across the platform wood. Scribbled in chalk beside the rooftop anemometer were the words, "the sky is a bass drum waiting for the rails to strike it."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric surreal visual of wooden taiko sticks wrapped in frayed silk lying abandoned on frosted zinc roof tiles, dawn twilight bleeding pale silver and lavender into the sky, cold industrial rail yards stretching out beneath rising white vapor.

